  "id": "DrugBank_DB00927 - MONDO_5412",
  "verbalization": "Famotidine, a proton pump inhibitor, is implicated in the pathogenesis of duodenal ulcer disease through multiple indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Famotidine may influence gastric ulcer development by modulating gastric acid secretion, as indicated by its indication for gastrin secretion abnormalities and its association with dexrabeprazole, a proton pump inhibitor. This implies that Famotidine could potentially reduce gastric acid production, which is a key factor in the development of duodenal ulcers. Additionally, the indirect link through vonoprazan, a proton pump inhibitor, further supports the notion that Famotidine may contribute to the prevention of duodenal ulcers by inhibiting acid secretion. The cumulative evidence suggests that Famotidine may exert a protective effect against duodenal ulcers through mechanisms involving acid suppression, though the exact role of Famotidine in this context remains partially elucidated. The observed relationships highlight the importance of acid suppression in the management of duodenal ulcers, but further mechanistic studies are needed to fully understand the role of Famotidine in this context.",
